IMPORT prelude

-- Medical Registration Act 1997 (Singapore) -- who may practise medicine, what an unregistered practitioner loses,
-- medical certificates, the traditional-medicine defence, titles, practising while suspended, fraud, ships' surgeons,
-- the duty to report an unfit colleague, and composition: ss 13 to 17 and 62 to 67.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Named by 48 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository, most of them without citing a
-- section: they rely on it to say who is a registered medical practitioner, which s 16 settles for "duly qualified". SCOPED to practice and discipline as they
-- reach a patient or a doctor. Not encoded: the Medical Council's constitution (Part 2), the registers and the
-- registration routes (Part 4), the accreditation boards (Parts 5 and 5A), the investigation, committee and
-- interim-order machinery of Part 7 except the limitation period and the Disciplinary Tribunal's orders.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Medical Registration Act 1997 -- practice`

§§ `13, 16 -- who may practise`

-- s 13: "a person must not practise medicine or do any act as a medical practitioner unless the person is registered
-- under this Act AND has a valid practising certificate". s 16: "legally qualified medical practitioner", "duly qualified
-- medical practitioner" and any like expression in ANY written law mean "a person who is registered under this Act and
-- has a valid practising certificate". s 59F(3): a suspended practitioner "is not regarded as being registered".
-- s 66: ships' surgeons treating crew and passengers on board are exempt from registration and practising certificates.
DECLARE Doctor HAS
    `is registered` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has a valid practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is suspended` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a ship's surgeon treating crew or passengers on board` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the doctor` IS A Doctor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is a duly qualified medical practitioner` IF
        (    `the doctor`'s `is registered`
         AND `the doctor`'s `has a valid practising certificate`
         AND NOT `the doctor`'s `is suspended`)
    OR  `the doctor`'s `is a ship's surgeon treating crew or passengers on board`

GIVEN `the doctor` IS A Doctor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is an unauthorised person` IF
    NOT `the person is a duly qualified medical practitioner` `the doctor`

§§ `14, 15 -- fees and certificates`

-- s 14: an unauthorised person "is not entitled to demand, claim, accept, receive or retain or sue for or recover by any
-- means any charge, fee ... for or in connection with any medical or surgical advice, service ... or ... any medicine
-- which the person has prescribed and supplied". The test is "at the time of rendering such services".
-- s 15: a certificate "required by any written law to be signed by a duly qualified medical practitioner ... is not
-- valid unless signed by a person who is registered ... and has a valid practising certificate".
--
-- A medical certificate signed by a registered doctor whose practising certificate has lapsed is not valid where a
-- written law requires one -- the sick-leave certificate under the Employment Act, for example. The employee who relied
-- on it is not told so by the text, and the text gives them no saving.
GIVEN `the doctor` IS A Doctor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person may recover fees for the treatment` IF
    `the person is a duly qualified medical practitioner` `the doctor`

GIVEN `the doctor` IS A Doctor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a statutory medical certificate signed by the person is valid` IF
        `the doctor`'s `is registered`
    AND `the doctor`'s `has a valid practising certificate`
    AND NOT `the doctor`'s `is suspended`

§§ `17 -- unauthorised practice`

-- s 17(1): an unauthorised person who (a) practises medicine, (b) wilfully and falsely pretends to be a duly qualified
-- practitioner, (c) practises under the title of "physician, surgeon, DOCTOR, licentiate in medicine or surgery,
-- bachelor of medicine, or medical practitioner" or anything implying a medical degree, (d) holds out as a medical
-- practitioner, or (e) contravenes s 13 or 14 -- $100,000 or 12 months; second conviction $200,000 or 2 years.
-- (2): defence if the defendant proves "(a) he practised a system of therapeutics according to MALAY, CHINESE OR INDIAN
-- method; and (b) he did not in any way represent himself as a duly qualified or registered medical practitioner."
--
-- The defence names three traditions. A practitioner of Thai, Japanese, Korean or Western herbal medicine, or of
-- homeopathy, is not within (2)(a) on the text.
DECLARE Tradition IS ONE OF
    `Malay`
    `Chinese`
    `Indian`
    `Thai`
    `Japanese`
    `Western herbal`
    `homeopathy`
    `none`

DECLARE PracticeFacts HAS
    `the practitioner` IS A Doctor
    `practises medicine or a branch of it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `uses the title doctor, physician or surgeon in practice` IS A BOOLEAN
    `represented themselves as a qualified or registered practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the tradition` IS A Tradition

GIVEN `the tradition` IS A Tradition
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tradition is within the section 17(2) defence` IF
    CONSIDER `the tradition`
    WHEN `Malay` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Chinese` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Indian` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the facts` IS A PracticeFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person commits the section 17 offence` IF
        `the person is an unauthorised person` (`the facts`'s `the practitioner`)
    AND (    `the facts`'s `practises medicine or a branch of it`
         OR  `the facts`'s `uses the title doctor, physician or surgeon in practice`
         OR  `the facts`'s `represented themselves as a qualified or registered practitioner`)
    AND NOT (    `the tradition is within the section 17(2) defence` (`the facts`'s `the tradition`)
             AND NOT `the facts`'s `represented themselves as a qualified or registered practitioner`)

§§ `63 to 65 -- registered doctors: suspension and titles`

-- s 63: a suspended practitioner who practises -- $10,000 or 2 years.
-- s 64: a registered practitioner must use only registered or approved qualifications and approved titles;
--      contravention "may be subject to disciplinary proceedings". No offence.
-- s 65: a registered practitioner not registered as a specialist must not practise under, or advertise, a specialist
--      title; the same for "family physician". Contravention "may be subject to disciplinary proceedings". No offence.
--
-- An unregistered person who calls themselves "doctor" in practice commits a $100,000 offence (s 17). A registered
-- doctor who falsely calls themselves a "cardiologist" commits no offence at all: the only consequence is discipline.
-- And a suspended doctor who practises is caught twice: by s 63 ($10,000 or 2 years) and, being "not regarded as being
-- registered" (s 59F(3)), by s 17 ($100,000 or 12 months).
DECLARE Misuse IS ONE OF
    `an unregistered person calling themselves doctor`
    `a registered doctor falsely claiming to be a specialist`
    `a registered doctor using an unapproved qualification`
    `a registered doctor falsely claiming to be a family physician`

GIVEN `the misuse` IS A Misuse
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the misuse is a criminal offence` IF
    CONSIDER `the misuse`
    WHEN `an unregistered person calling themselves doctor` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the misuse` IS A Misuse
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for the misuse` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the misuse`
    WHEN `an unregistered person calling themselves doctor` THEN 100000
    OTHERWISE 0

-- A suspended doctor who practises.
DECLARE SuspendedOffence IS ONE OF
    `section 63`
    `section 17`

GIVEN `the offence` IS A SuspendedOffence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for the suspended doctor` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `section 63` THEN 10000
    WHEN `section 17` THEN 100000

-- In months.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A SuspendedOffence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment for the suspended doctor` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `section 63` THEN 24
    WHEN `section 17` THEN 12

§§ `62 -- fraudulent registration`

-- s 62: knowingly procuring registration or a practising certificate by a false declaration; a wilful false register
-- entry; forging a certificate; using one known to be forged; buying, selling or fraudulently obtaining one --
-- $10,000 or 2 years.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for fraudulent registration` MEANS 10000

§§ `67 -- reporting an unfit colleague`

-- s 67(1): a practitioner "who treats or attends to another registered medical practitioner who is, in the opinion of
-- the medical practitioner treating or attending to him, unfit to practise ... by reason of his mental or physical
-- condition must inform the Medical Council". (2): failure -- disciplinary proceedings only.
--
-- The duty falls only on the TREATING doctor. A colleague who sees the same unfitness at work has no statutory duty.
DECLARE Observer HAS
    `is a registered practitioner treating or attending the colleague` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is of the opinion the colleague is unfit through mental or physical condition` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the observer` IS AN Observer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the observer must inform the Medical Council` IF
        `the observer`'s `is a registered practitioner treating or attending the colleague`
    AND `the observer`'s `is of the opinion the colleague is unfit through mental or physical condition`

§§ `66A -- composition`

-- s 66A(1): up to the lower of half the maximum fine or $5,000, for prescribed offences.
GIVEN `maximum fine` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most that can be collected on composition` MEANS
    IF `maximum fine` DIVIDED BY 2 LESS THAN 5000 THEN `maximum fine` DIVIDED BY 2 ELSE 5000
